{"ts": 1789717338.3029308, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted short features 31, 26 and 37 at equal weight, with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.08 and a standard deviation of 0.30. The in-sample Sharpe is 0.815, which does not clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.02. I expect little or none of that in-sample figure to persist.\n\n- **Single features look like noise.** With 5,000 days (about 20 years), the standard error on an annualized Sharpe is roughly 0.22. The largest single-feature Sharpe in absolute value was 0.54 (feature 31), about 2.4 standard errors. That is about the maximum you would expect from 40 features with no real signal. The next two (0.47 and 0.39) also match what noise alone would produce.\n- **The combination adds nothing beyond that.** Combining the top three gives 0.815, almost exactly the 0.81 you get from diversifying three independent noise Sharpes. I stopped after five combinations rather than keep searching to beat the bar, since further searching would only overfit.\n- **Why the forecast is near zero.** I shrank the mean to 0.08, allowing a small chance that some of the signal is real. The 0.30 standard deviation covers out-of-sample sampling noise, assuming a test period of similar length, plus uncertainty about the true value.\n\nSeparately, the Gmail and Google Calendar connectors need authorization in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. Neither was needed for this task."}
